<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alfred Deakin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1856">1856</a>–<a href="/years/1919">1919</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alfred Deakin was an Australian lawyer and politician from Victoria, a leader of the federation movement and the second prime minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. He served three terms between 1903 and 1910 and shaped the early Commonwealth through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protective-tariff">protective tariffs</a>, the arbitration system and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-australia-policy">White Australia policy</a>. Earlier, as a Victorian minister, he had pioneered irrigation and factory legislation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfred-deakin.jpg" alt="Alfred Deakin" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: National Library of Australia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Alfred_Deakin_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
